Findings and drift for the dispatcher (none fixed here): (1) The packed binary's fast path is about 1.4 s per prompt against about 0.24 s for plain perl. That is PAR start-up, k188's board/topic. (2) The Perl CLI's own load (about 240 ms, Moo via Skilletor::Error::JSON and friends) bounds every hook. A leaner prompt path would need Skilletor::JSON/Error to load lazily; a ticket if wanted. (3) A TS edge, maybe a bug, src not touched: stdin JSON that is no object (e.g. null) reaches runHook as is, so session-start answers 'skilletor: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'cwd')'. Perl treats non-object JSON as no input. In the same vein, TS passes a non-string truthy input.cwd to projectRootOf, while Perl takes only a non-empty string. (4) Engine error wording differs (pre-existing, not the hook's): a write error where ~/.claude/rules is a file reads 'mkpath failed for <path>: File exists' in Perl (Path::Tiny), not Node's errno text. 69-hooks.t asserts one line only. (5) docs/perl.md 'Known gaps' still says the hooks are not ported. It is prose and has foreign uncommitted edits in the tree, so I left it. (6) On Windows spawn_detached dies (k186), so a due prompt there answers one warning line. What codex/hermes do in Perl after this card: --harness codex|hermes is parsed and passed on, but a hook answers exactly as Claude Code's. There is no Codex rules context (TS adds it only for codex-targeting scopes, which the Perl engine refuses anyway), no Hermes opt-in line and no prompt-hook move (k149); that is k184/k185. cwd-changed and post-tool-use answer 'skilletor: hook <event> is not ported to the Perl CLI yet' and exit 0 (k182). cli.test.ts :705 is k182's.

